{
  "brief_rationale": "The doctor avoided online reassurance, recognized the diabetic-foot postoperative wound and bone-cement detachment as requiring urgent in-person evaluation, and safely bounded diabetes sick-day medication advice without fixed home dosing. The caregiver executed the handoff to the original hospital emergency/wound pathway, and the doctor dynamically adapted guidance after high glucose, poor intake, ED testing, and specialist involvement became known. No responsibility-chain error is evident; at least two high-order points, real-world pathway navigation and dynamic reprioritization, were actively completed with meaningful impact, supporting grade 5.",
  "closure_assessment": {
    "citations": [
      {
        "event_id": "lite_evt_00122",
        "explanation": "The doctor confirmed the ED/specialist pathway and kept further decisions with the onsite team rather than claiming online resolution.",
        "quote": "这个方向完全正确"
      },
      {
        "event_id": "lite_evt_00122",
        "explanation": "The doctor assigned diabetes medication decisions to hospital clinicians.",
        "quote": "完全等你们指示，不动任何药"
      },
      {
        "event_id": "lite_evt_00137",
        "explanation": "The caregiver reported specialist assessment and that the patient was not being sent home.",
        "quote": "不要回家等"
      }
    ],
    "executable": true,
    "explanation": "This is a valid open transition: the patient was already in the original hospital ED/wound pathway, medication control had been handed to onsite clinicians, and unresolved items such as admission, cultures, imaging, and possible debridement had responsible offline owners. The last family message remained unanswered, but the prior plan was safe and executable with the patient under active ED/specialty supervision.",
